<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Löwenstein-Wertheim</h1><p class="st-meta">1574 CE – 1806 CE · capital Wertheim</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Franconian county formed when a morganatic branch of the Wittelsbachs inherited Wertheim on the Main in 1574.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-lowenstein-wertheim">Löwenstein</a> descended from Elector <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-i-elector-palatine">Frederick I of the Palatinate</a> and his morganatic wife Clara Tott. Count Ludwig III of Löwenstein married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-ii-abbess-of-quedlinburg">Anna of Stolberg</a>, heiress to the county of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-wertheim">Wertheim</a>, and in 1574 the couple took possession of Wertheim, creating Löwenstein-Wertheim.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts ruled lands on the Main around <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-wertheim">Wertheim</a> and elsewhere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> from the castle above the town. In 1611 the family split into a Protestant line, later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-lowenstein-wertheim">Löwenstein</a>-Wertheim-Virneburg and then Freudenberg, and a Catholic line, Löwenstein-Wertheim-Rochefort, raised to princes in 1711, which shared Wertheim. The territory belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconian-circle">Franconian Circle</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1806 both lines were mediatised and their lands divided mainly between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. The princely families survive, and the ruined castle above <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-wertheim">Wertheim</a> and the palace at Kleinheubach remain their landmarks.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="county, 1574 CE – 1806 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">county</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:91.02%;width:4.62%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1574 CE – 1806 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Wertheim</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ludwig III of Löwenstein</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism, Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>East Franconian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Löwenstein-Wertheim, Löwenstein-Wertheim-Rochefort, Löwenstein-Wertheim-Virneburg</dd></dl></aside></div>
